<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kalaburagi</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gulbarga, Ahsanabad</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gulbarga, officially Kalaburagi since 2014, is a city in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ala-ud-din-bahman-shah">Alauddin Hasan Bahman Shah</a> made it the first capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate</a> in 1347, and it remained the capital until the court moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bidar">Bidar</a> in the 1420s. The Bahmani fort with its great domed mosque, the royal tombs and the shrine of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gesu-daraz">Gesu Daraz</a> date from that period.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kalaburagi.jpg" alt="Kalaburagi" width="320" height="221" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dayal, Deen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Great_Mosque_in_Gulbarga_Fort..jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He soon moved his capital to <b>Gulbarga</b>, which he renamed Ahsanabad, in the heart of the Deccan plateau.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Bijapur Sultanate was one of five states that emerged from the break-up of the Bahmani Sultanate, which had ruled the Deccan from <b>Gulbarga</b> and later Bidar since 1347.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bijapur-sultanate">Bijapur Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The capital moved several times: the early kings ruled from the Ellora region, Govinda III from Mayurakhandi, and Amoghavarsha I founded Manyakheta, modern Malkhed in <b>Kalaburagi</b> district, which remained the seat of government until 972.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashtrakuta-dynasty">Rashtrakuta dynasty · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
